fKeys\\|r\\.shadow\\|r\\.actor\\|r\\.queue\\|r\\.sprite\\|r\\.prop\\|r\\.decal\\|r\\.sky\\|r\\.text\\|r\\.w(\\|game.follow\\|game.shake\\|game.freeze\\|game.flash\\|lights.add\\|particles\\.\\(sparks\\|ring\\|bits\\|text\\|dust\\)\\|game.tween\\|game.script\\|E.font\\.\\|E.ui\\.\\|audio.define\\|A.define\\|music(\\|sting\\|game.debug\\|cornerArea\\|game.go(\\|isTouch\\|touch\" API-INDEX.md | head -120","description":"Grep API index for key engine names"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NWydqVGxjV4h38Ghj9mH53","type":"tool_result","content":"29:const hero = new E.Body({ x: 40, y: 40, r: 5 });\n39:const E = My3D2dge, map = new E.TileMap({ rows: ['#######', '#.....#', '#..RR.#', '#######'], legend: { '#': 1, R: 2 }, types: { 1: { h: 80 }, 2: { h: 40 } } });\n49:The line each section starts on, and the name its classes' instances go by: `E.Attack: slash` lists its members as `slash.start`.\n57:- 268 Input; E.Input: input\n69:- 638 Humanoid rig; E.Attack: slash, E.Combo: combo, E.Humanoid: rig\n87:- 1104 Ui; E.Dialog: talk, E.Menu: menu\n210:- `E.sprite(rows, colors = {}, scale = 1)`: Pixel sprite written as strings. Each character is one pixel; colors maps a character to a hex color; '.' and ' ' are transparent. scale (whole number) enlarges each pixel. const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }); Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, COIN) (depth sorted) or px.sprite(g, COIN, sx, sy).\n215:- `E.anim(frames, fps = 8, o = {})` †: frames to play: anim([spr1, spr2, spr3], fps = 8, { loop: true, pingpong: false }), or anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 }) for several frames side by side in one sprite's rows. Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, a, { t }) (t: its clock, seconds; the game's time by default); a.frame(t) is the sprite at time t, a.index(t) its number, a.duration one play (seconds)\n217:- `E.rotSprite(spr, a, steps = 16)` †: the sprite turned by angle a (radians; the way the world's angles turn, so a ship drawn facing east turns to its facing): nearest-neighbour, whole pixels, about its center, in `steps` steps a turn (16; 32 for a smoother one) and cached, as games of the era pre-rotated theirs. r.sprite's and px.sprite's rot option use it\n270:- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\n271:- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\n272:- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\n273:- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\n274:- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\n275:- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /)\n279:- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\n282:- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\n288:- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n289:- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\n292:- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\n316:- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n317:- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\n318:- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\n320:- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel)\n329:- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\n340:- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\n344:- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\n346:- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\n350:- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\n356:- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\n357:- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\n358:- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\n359:- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\n360:- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\n361:- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\n362:- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\n366:- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\n381:- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\n387:- `game.particles.bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`: n pieces of debris that fly, fall and bounce (colors: a list to pick from)\n389:- `game.particles.dust(x, y, z, n, o = {})`: n puffs of dust from (x, y, z) that drift and fade. o: { speed: 30, color, size }\n394:- `game.particles.ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur = .35, z = 0)`: a ring on the ground growing from radius r0 to r1 over dur seconds (shockwaves, landings)\n396:- `game.particles.sparks(x, y, z, n, dir = null, o = {})`: n bright sparks thrown from (x, y, z), every way or around angle dir (radians). o: { color, speed }\n397:- `game.particles.text(x, y, z, text, color = '#fff2c4', o = {})`: floating number or word: text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true }) (bounce: lands and holds, Final Fantasy style)\n407:- `r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: Queue a character. drawFn(g, ox, oy) must draw with (ox, oy) as the projected position of world point (x, y, z). The engine adds outline, rim light, hit flash, afterimages and x-ray silhouettes. opts: {outline=true, rim=true, flash=false, alpha=1, xray=false, ghost:{color, life, alpha, owner}, hides, bias, every, key, emissive (it stays bright under lighting, as r.queue's)}\n408:- `r.actor({ xray })`: true shows the actor's hidden part (a translucent tint with a 1-px outline, r.xrayColor and r.xrayOutline; a checker in E.style.trans 'dither') wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high, or anything queued with hides is drawn in front of it (not one rising less than E.HEIGHTS.level, 8, above its feet: the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall); xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors } picks the colors, and with actors: true other bodies drawn in front hide it too (a hero lost in a crowd keeps his shape)\n409:- `r.actor({ hides })`: true: this actor counts as an x-ray occluder even when no x-ray actor asked for actors\n410:- `r.actor({ ghost })`: ghost.owner (any {x, y, z}, usually the actor itself) keeps its afterimages sorted behind the live body\n411:- `r.actor({ every, key })`: every: n, key (any object, the character's own): its finished image is drawn again for n - 1 frames before drawFn runs again, at the place it is now (a background character animating at a lower rate, as classic games' townsfolk do: a phone's frame keeps the time for the ones that matter); not while it flashes, with a ghost, x-ray or GPU lighting\n412:- `r.actor({ wrap })`: [w, h], as r.sprite's: a world that wraps round shows the actor across each edge it crosses\n413:- `r.actorNow(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: draw an actor at once, over what is drawn so far, not sorted by depth (a pass of the game's own: a body falling into a void painted over the level), with r.actor's options (outline, flash, alpha, ...)\n418:- `r.decal(fn, o)`: draw on the floor before the queue. opts.emissive (0..1) makes it glow under GPU lighting\n426:- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit })), each where its pixels went (an actor's drawn bounds; others' box, or a body's size round its point). [] for none\n427:- `r.prop(name, x, y, z, o = {})` †: set dressing from E.prop: r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { size, color, flip, light: true, halo: true, bias, hides, footprint }) feet on the world point; flames animate and glow. light: true also adds a warm light to game.lights. A prop at least chest high (E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22: a pillar, a statue, a lamp) drawn in front of an x-ray actor shows the actor's silhouette through it; a lower one (grass, a crate, a barrel) doesn't; hides: true or false says so.\n428:- `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, o)`: queue a depth-sorted draw. fn(g) draws into g. opts: {bias, occluder, hides, xray, emissive, solid (hides a glow behind it), footprint} occluder: a wall (x-ray silhouettes show through it, it sorts as a block). hides: only lets x-ray silhouettes show through it (props, sprites, bodies), nothing else changes\n429:- `r.shadow(x, y, rad, alpha = .5, color = '#0c0818', z = 0)`: soft contact shadow under a character: a cached dithered ellipse, correct for the current view. z = height of the surface it falls on (0 = floor; the top of a block or platform otherwise)\n432:- `r.sky(colors, o = {})`: background of dithered color bands, top to bottom: r.sky(['#0b1030', '#3b2d6b', '#e0806a']). Call first in draw.\n433:- `r.sprite(x, y, z, spr, o = {})`: Queue a pixel sprite (from E.sprite) standing at world point (x, y, z). Depth sorted like an actor. opts: { flip, anchor: 'bottom' (feet on the point, default; E.shapeSprite's: its own feet) | 'center' | 'top', glow: 0..1, bias, flash, outline, alpha, xray, ghost (the last five use the actor path), t (an E.anim's clock: spr may be one), rot (radians, about its center: anchor 'center' unless given), steps (16), scale, wrap: [w, h] (a world that wraps round: a copy across each edge it crosses; w alone wraps x) }\n435:- `r.text(str, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o)`: HUD text in screen pixels, drawn on top and never lit: r.text('SCORE 120', 4, 4, '#fff', { align, scale, outline })\n436:- `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: text floating at a world point (names, prompts): r.textAt(x, y, z, 'OPEN', '#ffe08a')\n440:- `r.w(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> buffer pixel coords\n444:- `r.ix`, `r.iy`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame (a world point is at r.w(x, y, z) on the buffer)\n473:- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n474:- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\n478:- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n480:- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n481:- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n482:- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', 'iris', 'mosaic', 'swirl' or 'dissolve', each way taking opts.seconds (.4), round opts.at (iris and swirl: a world point { x, y, z }, which each scene's camera places, or [x, y] on the screen; its centre by default). With reduce motion each is a plain fade\n484:- `game.menu(name)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. Returns true when a menu opened\n496:- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits: a number (seconds), nothing (one step), a condition (a function, or E.until(fn)), a tween or script (until it is done), a generator (run as a script of its own, and waited for), E.all([...]) or E.any([...]) (each of these). It runs at once to its first yield. Cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials, attract modes. Returns the Script\n499:- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n502:- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera } (all optional). start() with nothing resumes a stopped loop. A headless game, or one made with loop: false, runs only by advance().\n503:- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n508:- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(target), keep (outlive the scene) }). ease: a name in E.ease ('outQuad' by default) or a function of u 0..1. It starts from the values the target has once its delay is over. Returns the Tween: tween.done, tween.cancel()\n514:- `game.audio`: chip-sound synthesizer: game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song)\n518:- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the screen's centre to where the focus sits (above a phone's touch buttons: [0, -40]); x, y: the camera's position in projected pixels (its top-left corner); vx, vy: how fast it is moving there; tx, ty, tz: the world point it is following (focus sets it); snap: true makes it jump there at the next step instead of gliding (a new level, a teleport); window: [w, h] screen pixels, a dead zone round the focus it moves in before the camera follows (Super Mario World's; [48, 0] for sideways only); snapY: true follows a followed body's height only when it lands (onGround) or nears the screen's edge (side-scrollers); frame(targets, { margin: 24, zoom: [min, max] or false }) keeps every one of them (anything with x, y, z) in view, centered between them and zoomed out (no further than min) when they spread apart, until frame(null); free: a free camera (strategy, sims, board games): true or { keys: true, edge: 10, drag: true, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }: the keys or a pad's stick pan it (input.move(), speed screen pixels a second), the mouse at the screen's edge (within edge pixels), a drag (input.pan(): a finger on open ground, a mouse's middle or right button) one to one; a zoom (wheel, pinch) zooms toward the pointer or the pinch; its focus keeps to bounds (world x, y); focus() still moves it; in every view and turn; null (as it starts): off\n519:- `game.debug = null`: a game's own handle for tools and tests: game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }. tools/check.mjs prints snapshot() and tools/replay.mjs logs it at the end\n530:- `game.input`: keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch, read as actions (pressed, down, move, ...)\n531:- `game.lights` †: canvas lighting (lights.enabled, lights.add in draw)\n567:- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n586:- `new E.Script(game, fn, o = {}) → script` †: a script, made by game.script: done (it returned, or was cancelled), value (what it returned), cancel(); another script can yield it\n591:- `new E.Tween(game, target, to, seconds, o = {}) → tween` †: a tween, made by game.tween: done (it ended, or was cancelled), cancel(); a script can yield it\n640:- `new E.Attack(spec = {}, over) → slash`: new E.Attack(spec) or new E.Attack('slash', { reach: 9 }): a name from E.MOVES plus overrides\n666:- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Characters\", lists the options); update(dt, state) each step, draw(g, ox, oy, view) inside r.actor. hat: 'cap' | 'band' | 'crown' | 'helmet' | 'pointed' (or { style, color }); hood: true (drawn in colors.hair)\n703:- `E.MOVES: slash, backslash, overhead, rising, thrust, spin, plunge, twohand, jab, cross, hook, uppercut, haymaker, elbow, ... (24)`: a library of named attacks with timing and body motion (anticipation, lunge, step, twist, hop). new E.Attack('overhead'), new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 }), new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut']) rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: E.move('roundhouse', .5) }) // any move at any moment, for previews and cutscenes The same move works on every build and in every view: heights are relative to the shoulders (rel: true), reaches scale with arm length, and horizontal swings turn into screen-plane swings in side views. A move's spec fields:\n713:- `new E.Blob(o = {}) → blob`: new E.Blob({ R: 6.5, colors, ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face }): a squash-and-stretch rig for slimes and jellies; blob.update(dt, state) each step, blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view) in an r.actor\n720:- `blob.hang`: roosting upside down\n733:- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], types, floorTex } (built in code: fill, setFloor; with no cells it starts as open floor).\n734:- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n735:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n736:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n737:- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]\n738:- `new E.TileMap({ floorMat, floorMats })`: a material for the floor, floorMats: { tag: material } for tagged cells (in place of floorTex)\n739:- `new E.TileMap({ block })`: ['water', 'lava'] floor tags nobody can walk on (or put block: true in a legend entry)\n740:- `new E.TileMap({ chunks })`: bake the floor in squares near the camera (true, false; by default a map of more than 128 x 128 cells)\n746:- `map.collide(b)`: push a circle { x, y, r } out of walls. If the body has a number z, walls lower than z + step (default 2) do not block it (it is standing or jumping above them). Returns true when it touched a wall.\n747:- `map.drawFloor(r)`: draw the baked floor (first thing in draw, after r.sky); walls come from queueWalls(r)\n773:- `map.wall(from, dir, o = {})` †: the wall face a body touches or faces: from `from`, in direction dir (an angle or [dx, dy]), the first point within reach whose top rises more than a step above its feet. { x, y (on the face), nx, ny (the face's normal, out toward the body), top (how high it rises there), bottom (the floor at its foot), facing (the angle into it) } or null. Cells give axis faces; a map.solid box, the face back along dir. o: { reach: from.r + 4 }\n852:- `level.draw(r)`: draw the level as the background layer: call right after r.sky() and before anything else\n865:- `level.touching(b, what)`: does the body box overlap a tile of this kind ('hazard', 'ladder', ...) or id?\n921:- `new E.Body(o = {}) → body`: new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, ... })\n931:- `body.hitWall = false`: it touched a wall this step\n1043:- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\n1050:- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n1054:- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n1057:- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\n1064:- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n1089:- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\n1102:- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stands it on its feet (foot: [x, y] in the sprite) unless an anchor is given\n1106:- `new E.Dialog(game, o = {}) → talk`: new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, place, color, nameColor, voice, bg, border, vars }): a dialog box that types its text; say() opens it, update(dt) and draw(r) run it\n1135:- `new E.Menu(game, items, o = {}) → menu`: Menu with a cursor (title screens, pause menus, shops, battle commands). const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', 'OPTIONS', { label: 'CONTINUE', disabled: !save }], { onPick(i, item) {...}, onCancel() {...} }); update: menu.update(dt) draw: menu.draw(r) opts: { x: 'center' | px, y: 'center' | px, title, box: true, color, pick: '#ffd36a', columns: 1 }\n1156:- `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, frac, color = '#e0463c', o = {})`: a bar (a meter: health, magic, stamina, a boss's): ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, '#e0463c')\n1157:- `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, o = {})`: window frame: ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, shadow, gradient }) bg: one color (drawn as a soft top-to-bottom gradient, like Final Fantasy windows) or [top, bottom] colors. gradient: false for a flat fill.\n1158:- `E.ui.counter(g, x, y, label, value, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: label + value, e.g. ui.counter(g, 4, 4, 'RUPEES', 12)\n1159:- `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max, o = {})`: row of hearts: hp and max in hearts (halves allowed, e.g. 2.5 of 3)\n1177:- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n1303:- `E.Car.collide(cars)`: bump every pair of cars that touch apart (equal weights, a little bounce)\n1340:- `E.FIGHT_KEYS: up, down, left, right, lp, hp, lk, hk, start, pause, confirm, cancel` †: the fighting preset (a scene's input): the moves and menu keys of E.Input.DEFAULT, light and heavy punch (U, I; a pad's X, Y), light and heavy kick (J, K; A, B)\n1352:- `fightselect.inputs` †: each side's E.Input\n1361:- `fightstage.touching()` †: no ladders on a stage\n1366:- `fighter.draw(r)` †: draw it (and its shots) in the scene: r.actor with its rig","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e95508dc-65c4-494d-9367-fd17dc92e8aa","uuid":"813e1f9d-8629-45ab-983f-080e9eb46173","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:49.101Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"29:const hero = new E.Body({ x: 40, y: 40, r: 5 });\n39:const E = My3D2dge, map = new E.TileMap({ rows: ['#######', '#.....#', '#..RR.#', '#######'], legend: { '#': 1, R: 2 }, types: { 1: { h: 80 }, 2: { h: 40 } } });\n49:The line each section starts on, and the name its classes' instances go by: `E.Attack: slash` lists its members as `slash.start`.\n57:- 268 Input; E.Input: input\n69:- 638 Humanoid rig; E.Attack: slash, E.Combo: combo, E.Humanoid: rig\n87:- 1104 Ui; E.Dialog: talk, E.Menu: menu\n210:- `E.sprite(rows, colors = {}, scale = 1)`: Pixel sprite written as strings. Each character is one pixel; colors maps a character to a hex color; '.' and ' ' are transparent. scale (whole number) enlarges each pixel. const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }); Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, COIN) (depth sorted) or px.sprite(g, COIN, sx, sy).\n215:- `E.anim(frames, fps = 8, o = {})` †: frames to play: anim([spr1, spr2, spr3], fps = 8, { loop: true, pingpong: false }), or anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 }) for several frames side by side in one sprite's rows. Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, a, { t }) (t: its clock, seconds; the game's time by default); a.frame(t) is the sprite at time t, a.index(t) its number, a.duration one play (seconds)\n217:- `E.rotSprite(spr, a, steps = 16)` †: the sprite turned by angle a (radians; the way the world's angles turn, so a ship drawn facing east turns to its facing): nearest-neighbour, whole pixels, about its center, in `steps` steps a turn (16; 32 for a smoother one) and cached, as games of the era pre-rotated theirs. r.sprite's and px.sprite's rot option use it\n270:- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\n271:- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\n272:- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\n273:- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\n274:- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\n275:- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /)\n279:- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\n282:- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\n288:- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n289:- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\n292:- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\n316:- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n317:- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\n318:- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\n320:- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel)\n329:- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\n340:- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\n344:- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\n346:- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\n350:- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\n356:- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\n357:- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\n358:- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\n359:- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\n360:- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\n361:- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\n362:- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\n366:- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\n381:- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\n387:- `game.particles.bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`: n pieces of debris that fly, fall and bounce (colors: a list to pick from)\n389:- `game.particles.dust(x, y, z, n, o = {})`: n puffs of dust from (x, y, z) that drift and fade. o: { speed: 30, color, size }\n394:- `game.particles.ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur = .35, z = 0)`: a ring on the ground growing from radius r0 to r1 over dur seconds (shockwaves, landings)\n396:- `game.particles.sparks(x, y, z, n, dir = null, o = {})`: n bright sparks thrown from (x, y, z), every way or around angle dir (radians). o: { color, speed }\n397:- `game.particles.text(x, y, z, text, color = '#fff2c4', o = {})`: floating number or word: text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true }) (bounce: lands and holds, Final Fantasy style)\n407:- `r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: Queue a character. drawFn(g, ox, oy) must draw with (ox, oy) as the projected position of world point (x, y, z). The engine adds outline, rim light, hit flash, afterimages and x-ray silhouettes. opts: {outline=true, rim=true, flash=false, alpha=1, xray=false, ghost:{color, life, alpha, owner}, hides, bias, every, key, emissive (it stays bright under lighting, as r.queue's)}\n408:- `r.actor({ xray })`: true shows the actor's hidden part (a translucent tint with a 1-px outline, r.xrayColor and r.xrayOutline; a checker in E.style.trans 'dither') wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high, or anything queued with hides is drawn in front of it (not one rising less than E.HEIGHTS.level, 8, above its feet: the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall); xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors } picks the colors, and with actors: true other bodies drawn in front hide it too (a hero lost in a crowd keeps his shape)\n409:- `r.actor({ hides })`: true: this actor counts as an x-ray occluder even when no x-ray actor asked for actors\n410:- `r.actor({ ghost })`: ghost.owner (any {x, y, z}, usually the actor itself) keeps its afterimages sorted behind the live body\n411:- `r.actor({ every, key })`: every: n, key (any object, the character's own): its finished image is drawn again for n - 1 frames before drawFn runs again, at the place it is now (a background character animating at a lower rate, as classic games' townsfolk do: a phone's frame keeps the time for the ones that matter); not while it flashes, with a ghost, x-ray or GPU lighting\n412:- `r.actor({ wrap })`: [w, h], as r.sprite's: a world that wraps round shows the actor across each edge it crosses\n413:- `r.actorNow(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: draw an actor at once, over what is drawn so far, not sorted by depth (a pass of the game's own: a body falling into a void painted over the level), with r.actor's options (outline, flash, alpha, ...)\n418:- `r.decal(fn, o)`: draw on the floor before the queue. opts.emissive (0..1) makes it glow under GPU lighting\n426:- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit })), each where its pixels went (an actor's drawn bounds; others' box, or a body's size round its point). [] for none\n427:- `r.prop(name, x, y, z, o = {})` †: set dressing from E.prop: r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { size, color, flip, light: true, halo: true, bias, hides, footprint }) feet on the world point; flames animate and glow. light: true also adds a warm light to game.lights. A prop at least chest high (E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22: a pillar, a statue, a lamp) drawn in front of an x-ray actor shows the actor's silhouette through it; a lower one (grass, a crate, a barrel) doesn't; hides: true or false says so.\n428:- `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, o)`: queue a depth-sorted draw. fn(g) draws into g. opts: {bias, occluder, hides, xray, emissive, solid (hides a glow behind it), footprint} occluder: a wall (x-ray silhouettes show through it, it sorts as a block). hides: only lets x-ray silhouettes show through it (props, sprites, bodies), nothing else changes\n429:- `r.shadow(x, y, rad, alpha = .5, color = '#0c0818', z = 0)`: soft contact shadow under a character: a cached dithered ellipse, correct for the current view. z = height of the surface it falls on (0 = floor; the top of a block or platform otherwise)\n432:- `r.sky(colors, o = {})`: background of dithered color bands, top to bottom: r.sky(['#0b1030', '#3b2d6b', '#e0806a']). Call first in draw.\n433:- `r.sprite(x, y, z, spr, o = {})`: Queue a pixel sprite (from E.sprite) standing at world point (x, y, z). Depth sorted like an actor. opts: { flip, anchor: 'bottom' (feet on the point, default; E.shapeSprite's: its own feet) | 'center' | 'top', glow: 0..1, bias, flash, outline, alpha, xray, ghost (the last five use the actor path), t (an E.anim's clock: spr may be one), rot (radians, about its center: anchor 'center' unless given), steps (16), scale, wrap: [w, h] (a world that wraps round: a copy across each edge it crosses; w alone wraps x) }\n435:- `r.text(str, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o)`: HUD text in screen pixels, drawn on top and never lit: r.text('SCORE 120', 4, 4, '#fff', { align, scale, outline })\n436:- `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: text floating at a world point (names, prompts): r.textAt(x, y, z, 'OPEN', '#ffe08a')\n440:- `r.w(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> buffer pixel coords\n444:- `r.ix`, `r.iy`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame (a world point is at r.w(x, y, z) on the buffer)\n473:- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n474:- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\n478:- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n480:- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n481:- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n482:- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', 'iris', 'mosaic', 'swirl' or 'dissolve', each way taking opts.seconds (.4), round opts.at (iris and swirl: a world point { x, y, z }, which each scene's camera places, or [x, y] on the screen; its centre by default). With reduce motion each is a plain fade\n484:- `game.menu(name)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. Returns true when a menu opened\n496:- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits: a number (seconds), nothing (one step), a condition (a function, or E.until(fn)), a tween or script (until it is done), a generator (run as a script of its own, and waited for), E.all([...]) or E.any([...]) (each of these). It runs at once to its first yield. Cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials, attract modes. Returns the Script\n499:- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n502:- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera } (all optional). start() with nothing resumes a stopped loop. A headless game, or one made with loop: false, runs only by advance().\n503:- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n508:- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(target), keep (outlive the scene) }). ease: a name in E.ease ('outQuad' by default) or a function of u 0..1. It starts from the values the target has once its delay is over. Returns the Tween: tween.done, tween.cancel()\n514:- `game.audio`: chip-sound synthesizer: game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song)\n518:- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the screen's centre to where the focus sits (above a phone's touch buttons: [0, -40]); x, y: the camera's position in projected pixels (its top-left corner); vx, vy: how fast it is moving there; tx, ty, tz: the world point it is following (focus sets it); snap: true makes it jump there at the next step instead of gliding (a new level, a teleport); window: [w, h] screen pixels, a dead zone round the focus it moves in before the camera follows (Super Mario World's; [48, 0] for sideways only); snapY: true follows a followed body's height only when it lands (onGround) or nears the screen's edge (side-scrollers); frame(targets, { margin: 24, zoom: [min, max] or false }) keeps every one of them (anything with x, y, z) in view, centered between them and zoomed out (no further than min) when they spread apart, until frame(null); free: a free camera (strategy, sims, board games): true or { keys: true, edge: 10, drag: true, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }: the keys or a pad's stick pan it (input.move(), speed screen pixels a second), the mouse at the screen's edge (within edge pixels), a drag (input.pan(): a finger on open ground, a mouse's middle or right button) one to one; a zoom (wheel, pinch) zooms toward the pointer or the pinch; its focus keeps to bounds (world x, y); focus() still moves it; in every view and turn; null (as it starts): off\n519:- `game.debug = null`: a game's own handle for tools and tests: game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }. tools/check.mjs prints snapshot() and tools/replay.mjs logs it at the end\n530:- `game.input`: keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch, read as actions (pressed, down, move, ...)\n531:- `game.lights` †: canvas lighting (lights.enabled, lights.add in draw)\n567:- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n586:- `new E.Script(game, fn, o = {}) → script` †: a script, made by game.script: done (it returned, or was cancelled), value (what it returned), cancel(); another script can yield it\n591:- `new E.Tween(game, target, to, seconds, o = {}) → tween` †: a tween, made by game.tween: done (it ended, or was cancelled), cancel(); a script can yield it\n640:- `new E.Attack(spec = {}, over) → slash`: new E.Attack(spec) or new E.Attack('slash', { reach: 9 }): a name from E.MOVES plus overrides\n666:- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Characters\", lists the options); update(dt, state) each step, draw(g, ox, oy, view) inside r.actor. hat: 'cap' | 'band' | 'crown' | 'helmet' | 'pointed' (or { style, color }); hood: true (drawn in colors.hair)\n703:- `E.MOVES: slash, backslash, overhead, rising, thrust, spin, plunge, twohand, jab, cross, hook, uppercut, haymaker, elbow, ... (24)`: a library of named attacks with timing and body motion (anticipation, lunge, step, twist, hop). new E.Attack('overhead'), new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 }), new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut']) rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: E.move('roundhouse', .5) }) // any move at any moment, for previews and cutscenes The same move works on every build and in every view: heights are relative to the shoulders (rel: true), reaches scale with arm length, and horizontal swings turn into screen-plane swings in side views. A move's spec fields:\n713:- `new E.Blob(o = {}) → blob`: new E.Blob({ R: 6.5, colors, ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face }): a squash-and-stretch rig for slimes and jellies; blob.update(dt, state) each step, blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view) in an r.actor\n720:- `blob.hang`: roosting upside down\n733:- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], types, floorTex } (built in code: fill, setFloor; with no cells it starts as open floor).\n734:- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n735:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n736:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n737:- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]\n738:- `new E.TileMap({ floorMat, floorMats })`: a material for the floor, floorMats: { tag: material } for tagged cells (in place of floorTex)\n739:- `new E.TileMap({ block })`: ['water', 'lava'] floor tags nobody can walk on (or put block: true in a legend entry)\n740:- `new E.TileMap({ chunks })`: bake the floor in squares near the camera (true, false; by default a map of more than 128 x 128 cells)\n746:- `map.collide(b)`: push a circle { x, y, r } out of walls. If the body has a number z, walls lower than z + step (default 2) do not block it (it is standing or jumping above them). Returns true when it touched a wall.\n747:- `map.drawFloor(r)`: draw the baked floor (first thing in draw, after r.sky); walls come from queueWalls(r)\n773:- `map.wall(from, dir, o = {})` †: the wall face a body touches or faces: from `from`, in direction dir (an angle or [dx, dy]), the first point within reach whose top rises more than a step above its feet. { x, y (on the face), nx, ny (the face's normal, out toward the body), top (how high it rises there), bottom (the floor at its foot), facing (the angle into it) } or null. Cells give axis faces; a map.solid box, the face back along dir. o: { reach: from.r + 4 }\n852:- `level.draw(r)`: draw the level as the background layer: call right after r.sky() and before anything else\n865:- `level.touching(b, what)`: does the body box overlap a tile of this kind ('hazard', 'ladder', ...) or id?\n921:- `new E.Body(o = {}) → body`: new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, ... })\n931:- `body.hitWall = false`: it touched a wall this step\n1043:- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\n1050:- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n1054:- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n1057:- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\n1064:- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n1089:- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\n1102:- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stands it on its feet (foot: [x, y] in the sprite) unless an anchor is given\n1106:- `new E.Dialog(game, o = {}) → talk`: new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, place, color, nameColor, voice, bg, border, vars }): a dialog box that types its text; say() opens it, update(dt) and draw(r) run it\n1135:- `new E.Menu(game, items, o = {}) → menu`: Menu with a cursor (title screens, pause menus, shops, battle commands). const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', 'OPTIONS', { label: 'CONTINUE', disabled: !save }], { onPick(i, item) {...}, onCancel() {...} }); update: menu.update(dt) draw: menu.draw(r) opts: { x: 'center' | px, y: 'center' | px, title, box: true, color, pick: '#ffd36a', columns: 1 }\n1156:- `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, frac, color = '#e0463c', o = {})`: a bar (a meter: health, magic, stamina, a boss's): ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, '#e0463c')\n1157:- `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, o = {})`: window frame: ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, shadow, gradient }) bg: one color (drawn as a soft top-to-bottom gradient, like Final Fantasy windows) or [top, bottom] colors. gradient: false for a flat fill.\n1158:- `E.ui.counter(g, x, y, label, value, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: label + value, e.g. ui.counter(g, 4, 4, 'RUPEES', 12)\n1159:- `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max, o = {})`: row of hearts: hp and max in hearts (halves allowed, e.g. 2.5 of 3)\n1177:- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n1303:- `E.Car.collide(cars)`: bump every pair of cars that touch apart (equal weights, a little bounce)\n1340:- `E.FIGHT_KEYS: up, down, left, right, lp, hp, lk, hk, start, pause, confirm, cancel` †: the fighting preset (a scene's input): the moves and menu keys of E.Input.DEFAULT, light and heavy punch (U, I; a pad's X, Y), light and heavy kick (J, K; A, B)\n1352:- `fightselect.inputs` †: each side's E.Input\n1361:- `fightstage.touching()` †: no ladders on a stage\n1366:- `fighter.draw(r)` †: draw it (and its shots) in the scene: r.actor with its rig","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NWydqVGxjV4h38Ghj9mH53","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
